# Never Sleep. Poll.

Every long-running thing here publishes a completion signal. Read it. Do not guess a duration and
sleep, and never sleep "to be safe" after a job has already reported done.

## The loop
1. Kick the job off.
2. **Immediately** read its signal — no wait before the first read.
3. If it is not done, read again straight away. The tool round-trip is the only delay there should
   be. Only when a job is genuinely long (a multi-minute build) may you wait, and then in short
   slices, never one long block.

## Signals to read
| Job | Signal |
|---|---|
| Edit-mode / play-mode tests | `get_test_job(job_id)` → `status`, `result.summary` |
| Compile / import | `read_console` errors, then `mcpforunity://editor/state` → `advice.ready_for_tools` |
| Tutorial E2E | `DevTutorialE2ETest.LastReport` (survives the domain reload), `IsDone`, `Passed` |
| Other dev harnesses | `DevE2ETest.Report` / `DevBotE2ETest.Report`, `[E2E]` console lines, `RESULT:` |
| Round/phase progress | `GameLoop.currentPhase`, `DevInput.Dump()` |
| Background shell | the terminal file, or the end-of-turn completion notification |

## Rules
- `AwaitShell` with no `shell_id` is a blind sleep. Do not use it to wait on Unity work.
- These runs are seconds, not minutes. A 15-second sleep on a 1-second job is a bug in your process.
- If a signal does not survive what you are doing, persist it — the console buffer is wiped by
  domain reload, which is why the E2E report is parked in `SessionState`.
- If polling shows no progress, diagnose with `execute_code` (read live state) instead of waiting
  longer. A stalled job will not unstall because you slept.
- Prefer self-stopping harnesses that set a done flag and exit Play mode themselves.

```
// ❌ BAD
run_tests(...); AwaitShell(35000); get_test_job(...)

// ✅ GOOD
run_tests(...); get_test_job(...)   // still running? call get_test_job again immediately
```
